Regenerating the golden vectors in python/rxsurvey.py
=====================================================

GOLDEN_FRAMES in python/rxsurvey.py is not hand-written and must not be edited by
hand. The frames come from SurveyEncodeFrame() itself, compiled for the host, so
the Python decoder is checked against the real encoder rather than against a
Python re-implementation of it -- which would agree with itself no matter how
wrong both were.

Regenerate whenever the wire format in lib/RxSurvey/SurveyProtocol.h changes.
Save the program below as gen.cpp somewhere outside the tree, then from src/:

    g++ -std=c++11 -Ilib/RxSurvey -o gen gen.cpp && ./gen

Paste the two hex lines into GOLDEN_FRAMES, keeping the order (full frame first,
gate report second), and re-run `python rxsurvey.py --selftest`. The field values
below are what the selftest asserts, so change both together or the assertions
stop testing the encoder and start testing themselves.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstring>
#include "SurveyProtocol.h"

static void dump(const uint8_t *p, const uint8_t n)
{
    for (uint8_t i = 0; i < n; i++) printf("%02x", p[i]);
    printf("\n");
}

int main()
{
    uint8_t payload[SURVEY_MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES];

    surveySample_t s[SURVEY_MAX_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME];
    for (uint8_t i = 0; i < SURVEY_MAX_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME; i++)
    {
        memset(&s[i], 0, sizeof(s[i]));
        s[i].chan1 = i;
        s[i].chan2 = (uint8_t)(i + 40);
        s[i].rssi1 = (int8_t)(-100 + i);
        s[i].rssi2 = (int8_t)(-95 + i);
        s[i].offsetQus = 200 / SURVEY_OFFSET_QUANTUM_US;
        s[i].packetRssi = (int8_t)(-40 - i);
        s[i].flags = (uint8_t)(SURVEY_SFLAG_GEMINI |
                               ((i & 1) ? SURVEY_SFLAG_PACKET_ON_RADIO2 : 0));
    }

    surveyFrameInfo_t full;
    memset(&full, 0, sizeof(full));
    full.flags = SURVEY_FLAG_ARMED | SURVEY_FLAG_DUAL_RADIO;
    full.seq = 50;
    full.reqOffsetQus = 200 / SURVEY_OFFSET_QUANTUM_US;
    full.enumRate = 4;
    full.bwKhz = 812;
    full.lnaGainDb = 12;
    full.channelCount = 80;
    full.startFreqKhz = 2400400;   // the real 2.4GHz FHSS grid
    full.stepKhz = 1000;
    full.dropped = 0;
    full.sampleCount = SURVEY_MAX_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME;
    dump(payload, SurveyEncodeFrame(payload, s, &full));

    surveyFrameInfo_t gate;
    memset(&gate, 0, sizeof(gate));
    gate.flags = SURVEY_FLAG_ARMED | SURVEY_FLAG_GATED_TIMER | SURVEY_FLAG_GATED_RATE;
    gate.seq = 51;
    gate.reqOffsetQus = 200 / SURVEY_OFFSET_QUANTUM_US;
    gate.enumRate = 4;
    gate.bwKhz = 500;
    gate.lnaGainDb = 12;
    gate.channelCount = 40;
    gate.startFreqKhz = 903500;    // the real FCC915 grid
    gate.stepKhz = 600;
    gate.dropped = 600;
    gate.sampleCount = 0;
    dump(payload, SurveyEncodeFrame(payload, 0, &gate));
    return 0;
}
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

test_rxsurvey.cpp itself needs none of this: it round-trips through both halves
of the codec in the same process, which catches a broken encoder/decoder pair
only if they break differently. The golden vectors are what catch them breaking
together, and they are the only check that the C and Python sides agree.
